Skyrim and its races have a rich cultural religious lore of not just the Nords, but every race in Tamriel. Vanilla Skyrim does not embrace this. This is the first religion mod that injects lore into your everyday choices and actions in a meaningful way. Your devotion will reveal itself in the journey your character takes.

[size=5][b]Main Features[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Customised religious paths and potentials for each race. Dunmer worship their ancestors and the Reclamations. A Nord can choose to worship their entire pantheon or accept an offer to devote themselves to a particular deity. Each with their own pantheon, deeds, and rhythms.
[*]Sixteen Daedric Princes await in Skyrim, and may take an interest. A pact with a Prince is a transaction: a boon, a price, and a stigma.
[*]Neglect and curses that answer a lapsed faith or a cursed one.
[*]The "Book of Days" journal - this tool and immersion feature records your standing and the history of how you got there.
[*]Two paths. Pilgrim's Path is authored for Requiem balance. Wayfarer's Path is a gentler choice for people who hate survival mode. Switch whenever you like.
[*]Full Papyrus source ships with the mod.
[/list]

[size=5][b]Optional Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Prisma UI - strongly recommended. The Devotion panel is drawn with it. Devotion ships the panel's own views and its bridge, but not the framework itself. Without Prisma UI the mod plays normally and the MCM still reports your standing; you simply do not get the panel, its Book of Days view, or its toasts.
[*]A survival or needs mod. Survival Mode and SunHelm both work. Devotion plays fully without one, but the outdoor, weather, and rest blessings are more meaningful when cold and hunger actually matter.
[/list]

[size=5][b]Installation[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Install every requirement above first.
[*]Install this archive with your mod manager as normal, then enable Devotion.esp. To install by hand, extract the contents into your Skyrim Special Edition Data folder.
[*]Place Devotion.esp by hand rather than letting LOOT decide (which should never be run on a large premade list). Late in the load order is correct.
[*]Start a new game, or use a save that has never had Devotion installed. Adding Devotion partway through a playthrough is not supported.
[/list]

[size=5][b]Uninstalling[/b][/size]

Make a throwaway save. Open the MCM, go to Player, then Maintenance, and run Prepare for uninstall. Make a fresh save, then remove the plugin.

This is best-effort rather than a clean save. Removing the plugin without running it first will strand abilities and orphaned scripts.

[size=5][b]Compatibility[/b][/size]

Devotion replaces other religion systems. Run it instead of another deity-worship or religion overhaul, not alongside one. Stacking two religion systems is not supported.

Devotion does not touch meshes, cells, or shrine activators, so combat, world, gear, NPC, race, faction, and loot mods are all fine, as are shrine retextures and lighting.

Devotion is balanced with Requiem's scale in mind. List-specific compatibility patches for major overhaul setups are in development. If you run a heavy overhaul or a curated modlist, check the compatibility notes for your setup before installing.

[size=5][b]AI Transparency Statement[/b][/size]

As a working professional in AI and Data, I work with AI and data tools in my everyday life. As such, I believe strongly in keeping my AI use transparent so people can decide if they are comfortable with what I have produced.

Devotion was built with AI coding assistants, working under human direction and review. The scripts were drafted using this method, but creative content was authored by myself. Changes are checked by reading the built records back out of the plugin and testing behavior in play, and the project holds to the standard that a feature is not finished until it is observed working as intended.

The deity glyphs drawn in the Devotion panel are vector artwork built from hand-specified coordinates in code, not generative images.

